On Mar 15, 8:35=A0am, James Aguilar <aguilar.ja...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hey guys,
>
> I'm trying to write a program that parses Nethack and does some stuff
> with it. (One of my ideas, for example, is to make a program that will
> automatically price-ID items on your behalf when you type in a certain
> extended command.) Anyway, I've gotten to the point where I am mostly
> in sync internally with the current state of the terminal, except one
> problem.
>
> I noticed that even though I have DECGraphics turned off, Nethack is
> sending some commands to the terminal that come from DEC's private
> command set. They troublesome one right now is "CSI [ ? 47 h", which
> switches to the alternate screen buffer, and "CSI [ ? 47 l", its
> sister, that switches back to the normal buffer. I'm currently not
> parsing anything from the extended set, so these commands get ignored.
> But in my testing, when I dump the contents of my program's buffer, it
> has some junk left over from the startup screen (when the copyright
> information was displayed).
DECgraphics has nothing to do with the actual command set used;
instead, it's a convention that between SI and SO characters, a
different character set is used that contain graphics. That's all it
affects - graphics. (IBMgraphics, likewise, uses characters with codes
above 128 with the interpretation given to them by one of the IBM
codepages; IIRC, 437, but I may be wrong on that.) So turning
DECgraphics off won't give you the terminal behaviour you need. You
could try, instead, setting your TERM environment variable to tell the
terminal emulation library that you're using a different sort of
terminal.

> I can't tell for sure, but I think the ordinary terminal commands do
> not erase that area, so the issue is that Nethack thinks the copyright
> notice is automatically erased by switching back to the normal buffer,
> but my program doesn't because those commands aren't implemented. I
> can implement the commands easily enough (in practice that just means
> I'm going to clear the entire screen). But I am wondering if it should
> be considered a bug that Nethack uses DEC-specific commands when
> DECgraphics is ostensibly turned off. I don't know very much about
> terminals, so it's possible I'm completely off-base here.
Not a bug, because that's not what DECgraphics means. (It would be a
bug if it used commands specific to one terminal in a situation where
the terminal identified, via response to the relevant command and
environment variable, as a different terminal.)

